{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.Wedderburn","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.Wedderburn","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Hecke.Algebra","lean:module:QOU.Hecke.MarkovTrace","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.List.Sort","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Trace","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Hom","lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.IdentityIContentDecomposition","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SYTHookGNW"],"declarations":36,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.frobPairing_block_decomp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.frobPairing_block_decomp","kind":"theorem","line":340,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L340-L365","doc":"**(§9.4 L-3, block decomposition)** — the Frobenius pairing splits\nover the Wedderburn blocks with no residue:\n`⟨a,b⟩_F = Σ_λ ⟨a·π_λ, b·π_λ⟩_F`. Together with\n`frobPairing_block_orthogonal` this is the per-λ block-orthogonality\n`⟨·,·⟩_F = ⊕_λ ⟨·,·⟩_F^λ` of §9.4 L-3, derived from the resolution of\nidentity (`trace_exhaustive`) + centrality + idempotency — conditional\non the same `WedderburnDecomp` class as L-2's `pi_mul_pi`, with the\nclass-axiom discharge (Mathas §3.43) remaining the L-1/L-2 debt. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.frobPairing_block_orthogonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.frobPairing_block_orthogonal","kind":"theorem","line":328,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L328-L338","doc":"**(§9.4 L-3, cross-block vanishing)** — the Frobenius pairing of\nelements in *distinct* Wedderburn blocks vanishes:\n`⟨a·π_λ, b·π_μ⟩_F = 0` for `λ ≠ μ`. Derived from centrality\n(`pi_mul_shift`) + Kronecker orthogonality (`pi_mul_pi`); no new\naxiom beyond the `WedderburnDecomp` class. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.frobPairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.frobPairing","kind":"def","line":320,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L320-L325","doc":"The Hecke-side **Frobenius pairing** `⟨a,b⟩_F = tr_M(a·b)`\n(cf. `def:frobenius-hopf-substrate`: `⟨a,b⟩_F = ∫_A(a·b)` with the\nMarkov trace as the integral). Symmetric by the Markov cyclicity\naxiom (M2). `n` and `q` are explicit because they are not inferable\nfrom `a b : A` alone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.pi_mul_shift","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.pi_mul_shift","kind":"theorem","line":309,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L309-L317","doc":"Auxiliary product shift: `(a·π_λ)·(b·π_μ) = (a·b)·(π_λ·π_μ)`, from\ncentrality of the idempotents. The noncommutative bookkeeping behind both\nL-3 theorems below. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.trace_decomposition","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.trace_decomposition","kind":"theorem","line":286,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L286-L307","doc":"**Wedderburn decomposition of the Markov trace, derived**:\n`tr_M(x) = Σ_λ y_λ · χ^λ(x)`, the sum running over `WD.all_parts` (the\ncomplete set of irreducibles).\n\nTwo inputs, neither of them the conclusion: `complete` (via\n`trace_exhaustive`, which contributes the unweighted sum) and `block_scaling`\n(which contributes the weights, one block at a time). Note where each enters —\nthe `y_λ` are *not* a consequence of completeness, which is why assuming the\nwhole identity was never necessary, and they are *not* free either, which is\nwhy the honest axiom is the block scaling.\n\nPreviously this projected a `trace_decomp` field, i.e. proved nothing; see\n`block_scaling`'s docstring for what that field asserted and why it was worse\nthan redundant. The general form of this argument, stated on a bare\n`AddMonoidHom` with no Hecke structure at all, is\n`QOU.QBeta.WedderburnTraceDecomposition.trace_eq_sum_weighted_characters`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.trace_exhaustive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.trace_exhaustive","kind":"theorem","line":266,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L266-L284","doc":"**Exhaustiveness of the Markov trace over the Wedderburn blocks** —\n`tr_M(x) = Σ_λ tr_M(x · π_λ)`, with NO residue.  The machine-checked\n\"we account for everything\": *derived* from the resolution of identity\n`Σ_λ π_λ = 1` (`WD.complete`) via left-distributivity and `R`-linearity\nof the trace — it does NOT re-assume a decomposition.  The sum runs over\nthe complete set `WD.all_parts` (the `p(n)` partitions of `n`); there are\nno other blocks.  This lemma needs only `complete`; `trace_decomposition`\nbelow adds `block_scaling` to it and gets the `y_λ`-weighted form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.pi_mul_pi","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.pi_mul_pi","kind":"theorem","line":242,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L242-L264","doc":"**(§9.4 L-2) Kronecker orthogonality of the central idempotents** —\n`π_λ · π_μ = δ_{λ,μ} · π_λ`. Packages the `idempotent` and `orthogonal`\naxioms into one relation. The statement is keyed on `lam = mu` (matching the\nKronecker `δ_{λ,μ}` and independent of `Partition`'s internal representation);\nthe `@[ext]` proof-irrelevance step (equal `parts` ⇒ equal `Partition`) is used\nonly internally to bridge to the `parts`-keyed `orthogonal` axiom. Makes the\nblock algebra directly usable.\n\nThe *standing* §9.4 debt is to **discharge** the `orthogonal` axiom itself\n(and `y_lambda` uniqueness, L-1) from the Hecke structure via Schur\ncharacter-orthogonality — Mathas (1999) §3.43, not yet in Mathlib. This lemma\nis the immediate consequence of the current axioms, not that discharge. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.yLambda_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.yLambda_unique","kind":"theorem","line":223,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L223-L239","doc":"**Uniqueness of the weight.** Two decompositions agreeing on the block\nidempotent agree on `y_λ`, provided the block dimension is not a zero divisor.\n\nThe `blockDim` hypothesis the 2026-08-30 morning version of this theorem\ncarried is **gone**, and its absence is the content: `blockDim` is now a\nfunction of `λ` alone, so two decompositions cannot disagree about it. What\nremains is a hypothesis about `R` (no zero divisors) and about the value\n`(f^λ : R)`, not about the decompositions. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.yLambda_determined","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.yLambda_determined","kind":"theorem","line":207,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L207-L220","doc":"**`y_λ` is determined.** `block_scaling` at `x = 1`, with `χ^λ(1) = f^λ`:\n\n  `tr_M(π_λ) = y_λ · f^λ`.\n\nThe weight is no longer a free parameter: it is the block trace against the\nblock dimension. Contrast `QOU.Hecke.RankOneDegeneracy`, where against the\nprevious class every nonzero rational was realisable as `y_λ` at every\npartition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.chi_lambda_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.chi_lambda_one","kind":"theorem","line":197,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L197-L205","doc":"`χ^λ(1) = f^λ`: the block character at the identity is the block dimension.\n\nThis is the rigidity the `AlgHom` upgrade buys, and the reason `y_λ` becomes\npinned below. A free `chi_lambda` field could take any value at `1`; an algebra\nmap cannot, because `ρ_λ 1 = 1` and the trace of an identity matrix is its\nsize. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.chi_lambda","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.chi_lambda","kind":"def","line":192,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L192-L195","doc":"The **block character** `χ^λ`, now *computed* as the matrix trace of the\nblock representation rather than carried as a free field. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.block_scaling","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.block_scaling","kind":"theorem","line":181,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L181-L181","doc":"**Block scaling.** The Markov trace restricted to block `λ` is the block\ncharacter scaled by the central coefficient: `tr_M(x · π_λ) = y_λ · χ^λ(x)`.\n\nThis is the sentence in this class's header docstring — *\"The Markov trace's\nrestriction to each block is the matrix trace scaled by the central\ncoefficient `y_λ`\"* — which was prose and is now a field. It is where the\n`y_λ` actually enter, and it is a **hypothesis**, not the conclusion:\n`trace_decomposition` derives `tr_M(x) = Σ_λ y_λ · χ^λ(x)` from it together\nwith `complete`, and neither input is that identity.\n\nIt replaces a field `trace_decomp` that asserted the conclusion directly, over\nthe terms `tr_M(x · π_λ)` rather than `χ^λ(x)` — applying `y_λ` on top of a\nquantity that already carries it. That field was not merely redundant with\nthe derived `trace_exhaustive`: the two together forced `(1 − y_μ) · tr_M(π_μ)\n= 0` for every block, i.e. every weight trivial or its block trace zero.\nMachine-checked as `weights_inert` / `weight_one_or_block_trace_zero` in\ncommit `9e8b712fa2`, which is where those two theorems can still be run; they\nare deleted here along with the field they were about. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.rho","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.rho","kind":"def","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L161-L161","doc":"The `λ`-block representation `ρ_λ : A → M_{f^λ}(R)`, as an **algebra\nhomomorphism**.\n\nThis replaces a free field `chi_lambda : Partition n → A → R`, and the\nupgrade is the point: an `AlgHom` satisfies `ρ_λ 1 = 1`, so the block\ncharacter has `χ^λ(1) = f^λ` forced. With `χ^λ` free, `block_scaling` was an\nequation on a product of two unknowns and pinned neither factor; with `ρ_λ`\nan algebra map it pins `y_λ` against `blockDim` (`yLambda_determined`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.complete","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.complete","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L152-L152","doc":"**Resolution of identity (completeness).** The central idempotents\nsum to `1`: `Σ_{λ ∈ all_parts} π_λ = 1`.  This is the machine-checked\n\"we account for everything\" — by Wedderburn–Artin the partitions of `n`\nindex ALL irreducibles (Mathas 1999 §3.43), so the blocks exhaust the\nalgebra with no residue.  `trace_exhaustive` below is the *derived*\nunweighted decomposition that rests on this partition of unity; the `y_λ`\nenter one level down, through `block_scaling`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.all_parts","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.all_parts","kind":"def","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L144-L144","doc":"The complete set of partitions (all irreducible representations\nof `H_n(q)`, one for each partition `λ ⊢ n`). This equals the full\nset `{λ | λ ⊢ n}` of Young diagrams of `n`; by the Wedderburn–Artin\ntheorem there are no other irreducibles over a generic base ring. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.y_lambda","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.y_lambda","kind":"def","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L139-L139","doc":"Markov-trace central coefficient y_λ. By\nprop:y-lambda-recipe-from-partition this is a rational function\nin q given by `s_λ(1, Q, ..., Q^{r-1}) / Σ d_μ s_μ(...)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.central","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.central","kind":"theorem","line":135,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L135-L135","doc":"Each `π_λ` is **central**: `π_λ ∈ Z(H_n(q))`. This was always the\nclaim of this class (see the docstring above: \"central idempotent\nπ_λ ∈ Z(H_n(q))\") and of the §9.1 partition-respecting table\n(\"Central idempotents π_λ (orthogonal, central) — asserted (class\naxiom)\"); encoding it as a field closes the docstring-vs-fields gap\n(the `proof-claims-unencoded` pattern) and unlocks the L-3 per-λ\nFrobenius block-orthogonality below as *derived* theorems. Its\ndischarge from the Hecke structure is the same Mathas (1999) §3.43\ndebt as `orthogonal` (§9.4 L-2). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.orthogonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.orthogonal","kind":"theorem","line":124,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L124-L124","doc":"Distinct idempotents are orthogonal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.idempotent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.idempotent","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L122-L122","doc":"Each pi_lambda is idempotent. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.pi_lambda","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.pi_lambda","kind":"def","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L120-L120","doc":"Central idempotent for partition λ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp.mk","kind":"ctor","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L115-L115","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.WedderburnDecomp","kind":"class","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L102-L182","doc":"Wedderburn–Artin decomposition of `H_n(q)`. An instance asserts:\n- For each partition λ ⊢ n, a central idempotent `pi_lambda λ`;\n- The idempotents are mutually orthogonal and sum to 1;\n- Each block `pi_lambda λ · A · pi_lambda λ` is isomorphic to\n  `M_{f^λ}(R)` (matrix algebra of size f^λ);\n- The Markov trace's restriction to each block is the matrix trace\n  scaled by the central coefficient `y_λ`.\n\nFor brevity we store only the central idempotents + scalar\ncoefficients here; the matrix-algebra isomorphisms are captured\nabstractly via a representation `rho_lambda`.\n","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.blockDim_eq_numSYT","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.blockDim_eq_numSYT","kind":"theorem","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L88-L100","doc":"**The block dimension IS the number of standard Young tableaux of shape `λ`.**\n\n`blockDim` is defined by the hook-length *formula*; this identifies it with the\ncombinatorial object that formula computes, via the corpus's sorry-free general\nFrame–Robinson–Thrall theorem.\n\nBoth hypotheses FRT needs come free from the `Partition n` structure — `parts` is\nweakly decreasing (`lam.decreasing`) and its entries are positive\n(`lam.positive`) — so there is nothing left to assume. This is what upgrades\n`blockDim` from a chosen formula to `f^λ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.blockDim","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.blockDim","kind":"def","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L63-L86","doc":"**The block dimension `f^λ`, computed from the partition.**\n\n`dimHLF` is the Frame–Robinson–Thrall hook-length dimension `n! / ∏_c h_c`\n(`QOU.BraidKnot.IdentityI`). This is a plain function of `λ`, not a field: it is\nwhat closes the last freedom the class had. Before 2026-08-30 `blockDim` was a\nfree `Partition n → ℕ`, so `y_λ` was pinned only *relative* to it and every\n`1/d` remained realisable.\n\n**This IS the SYT count, and that is a theorem here — not a commitment.**\nFrame–Robinson–Thrall (`dimHLF λ = #SYT(λ)`) is proved in this corpus at general\n`n`, sorry-free: `QOU.Mathlib.SYTEnum.numSYTenum_eq_dimHLF`, via the\nGreene–Nijenhuis–Wilf route (corner recursion + hook-summation identity, combined\nby strong induction). `blockDim_eq_numSYT` below discharges it against a\n`Partition n`, whose `decreasing` and `positive` fields supply exactly the two\nhypotheses FRT needs, so nothing is left assumed.\n\n**Corrected 2026-08-30.** An earlier version of this docstring said the general\ntheorem was \"open here\" and that the class committed to the FRT *formula* without\nproving FRT. Both halves were false. The error came from trusting\n`SYTHookFormulaRows.lean`'s header, which is itself stale and is fixed in the\nsame commit — the corpus, not a source file's status note, is the authority\n(AGENTS.md §\"Before declaring open\"). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.SpechtDimension.positive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.SpechtDimension.positive","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L61-L61","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.SpechtDimension.dim","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.SpechtDimension.dim","kind":"def","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L60-L60","doc":"Specht module dimension f^λ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.SpechtDimension.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.SpechtDimension.mk","kind":"ctor","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L58-L58","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.SpechtDimension","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.SpechtDimension","kind":"class","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L55-L61","doc":"The hook-length formula: f^λ = n! / Π hook(c). The hook lengths\nthemselves are computed in `Mathlib.Combinatorics.YoungTableau` (or\nre-derived locally); this is the abstract dimension predicate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.Partition.decreasing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.Partition.decreasing","kind":"theorem","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L53-L53","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.Partition.positive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.Partition.positive","kind":"theorem","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L52-L52","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.Partition.sum_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.Partition.sum_eq","kind":"theorem","line":51,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L51-L51","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.Partition.parts","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.Partition.parts","kind":"def","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L50-L50","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.Partition.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.Partition.mk","kind":"ctor","line":49,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L49-L49","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.Partition.ext_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.Partition.ext_iff","kind":"theorem","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L48-L48","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.Partition.ext","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.Partition.ext","kind":"theorem","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L48-L48","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.Partition","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.Partition","kind":"structure","line":45,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Wedderburn.lean#L45-L53","doc":"A partition `λ ⊢ n` represented as a non-increasing tuple of\npositive integers summing to `n`. We use `List ℕ` with explicit\npredicates for partition validity. ","signatureChunk":0}]}